<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.13.2: The Linear Term in a Quadratic Expression</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01968</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.13.2: The Linear Term in a Quadratic Expression</md:title>
    <md:uuid>7a6b0c4a-6811-4b6d-9f2c-44f2ada31751</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section>
<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph \(y=x^2\), and then experiment with adding different linear terms (for example, \(x^2+4x\), \(x^2+20x\), \(x^2-50x\)). Record your observations.</para>
    <para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>The graph still looks like a quadratic function when adding a linear term but it "shifts" both horizontally and vertically. Adding a positive multiple of \(x\) to \(x^2\) shifts it down and to the left while adding a negative multiple of \(x\) shifts it down and to the right.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><para>Use your observations from question 1 to answer questions 2 – 5 and complete the table.</para>
<table class="os-raise-wideequaltable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>equation</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>\(x\)-intercepts</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>\(x\)-coordinate of vertex</emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(y=x^2+6x\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>2a. _____</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>2b. _____</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(y=x^2-10x\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>3a. _____</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>3b. _____</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(y=-x^2+50x\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>4a. _____</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>4b. _____</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(y=-x^2-36x\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>5a. _____</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>5b. _____</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the \(x\)-intercepts and the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=x^2+6x\).</item>
</list>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> What are the \(x\)-intercepts for the equation \(y=x^2+6x\)? </para>
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\((0,0)\),\((-6,0)\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><section class="check-understanding"><title>Activity</title><exercise data-content-id="413dea93-428c-49a9-85b7-98afca31ae02"><problem><para>
      <para> What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=x^2+6x\)? </para>
        <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
    </para></problem><solution><para>-3</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is -3.</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the \(x\)-intercepts and the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=x^2-10x\).</item>
</list>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> What are the \(x\)-intercepts for the equation \(y=x^2-10x\)? </para>
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\((0,0)\),\((10,0)\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="909d6029-1695-4a0b-94eb-e5abf4758dd3"><problem><para>
      <para> What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=x^2-10x\)? </para>
        <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
    </para></problem><solution><para>5</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is 5.</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the \(x\)-intercepts and the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=-x^2+50x\).</item>
</list>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> What are the \(x\)-intercepts for the equation \(y=-x^2+50x\)? </para>
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\((0,0)\),\((50,0)\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="4d51c3c1-2de5-4dcf-af08-be98d21c5303"><problem><para>
      <para> What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=-x^2+50x\)? </para>
        <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
    </para></problem><solution><para>25</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is 25.</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the \(x\)-intercepts and the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=-x^2-36x\).</item>
</list>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> Find the \(x\)-intercepts for the equation \(y=-x^2-36x\). </para>
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\((0,0)\),\((-36,0)\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="1ed3a45d-fc40-408f-8981-6afb3ff574a4"><problem><para>
      <para> What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=-x^2-36x\)? </para>
        <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
    </para></problem><solution><para>-18</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is -18.</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><para>Some quadratic expressions have no linear terms. For questions 6 – 7, find the \(x\)-intercepts and the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph representing each equation:</para>
<para>\(y=x^2-25\)</para>
<para>\(y=x^2+16\)</para>
<para>(Note it is possible for the graph to not intersect the \(x\)-axis.) If you get stuck, try graphing the equations.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the \(x\)-intercepts and the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=x^2-25\).</item>
</list>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> What are the \(x\)-intercepts for the equation \(y=x^2-25\)? </para>
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\((5,0)\),\((-5,0)\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="9038b5dc-8f68-4b47-89af-ac89342ac1a8"><problem><para>
      <para> What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=x^2-25\)? </para>
        <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
    </para></problem><solution><para>0</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is 0.</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the \(x\)-intercepts and the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=x^2+16\).</item>
</list>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> What are the \(x\)-intercepts for the equation \(y=x^2+16\)? </para>
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>There are no \(x\)-intercepts.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="49956ae6-e262-433d-a091-048ffac06c56"><problem><para>
      <para> What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=x^2+16\) ? </para>
        <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </para></problem><solution><para>0</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is 0.</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
